# Digital roots

This file is a literate Avin program: a Markdown document whose lines indented four columns are
the program, and everything else, this paragraph included, is prose the compiler never reads.
Build and run it like any other source file:

```
avin run digits.lavin
avin tangle digits.lavin           the program alone, the prose stripped
avin weave digits.lavin -o digits.html
```

## Summing digits

A number's digits are summed by taking the last one off with `% 10` and dropping it with `/ 10`,
until nothing is left.

    digitSum(n: int) -> int
      var rest = n
      var sum = 0
      while rest > 0 do
        sum += rest % 10
        rest /= 10
      sum

## The digital root

Summing the digits of the sum, and again, until one digit is left, gives the *digital root*. The
loop below is the whole definition:

    digitalRoot(n: int) -> int
      var r = n
      while r >= 10 do
        r = digitSum(r)

Prose may stand between two lines of one function: the next line carries on at the indentation the
body left off at.

      r

## Checking it

The digital root of a positive number is also `1 + (n - 1) % 9`, which the program confirms for
every number below a thousand before printing a few.

    main()
      var n = 1
      var agree = true
      while n < 1000 do
        if digitalRoot(n) != 1 + (n - 1) % 9 then agree = false
        n += 1
      println(s"the two definitions agree below 1000: $agree")
      println(s"digital root of 2026: ${digitalRoot(2026)}")
      println(s"digital root of 987654321: ${digitalRoot(987654321)}")
